From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:52:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: make DrumBrake build again

DrumBrake, V8's Wasm interpreter, is the only way to run WebAssembly
without JIT: the tvOS port enables it, and a sideloaded iPhone app has
no JIT either. Upstream CI does not build it, and it fell behind two
API changes (V8 main on 2026-10-27 has these three files unchanged):

- TrustedManaged<T>::raw() now takes a DisallowGarbageCollection
  scope. Where a DirectHandle keeps the object alive across a possible
  GC (Execute() and friends), use unsafe_raw(), as wasm-objects-inl.h
  does. GetInterpretedStack() and GetFunctionIndex() only hold Tagged
  pointers and cannot GC, so they open a DisallowGarbageCollection
  scope and call raw(no_gc).
- TrustedCast<T> now only accepts trusted types. DrumBrake used it on
  ordinary heap objects (WasmTableObject, WasmArray, Map, HeapObject,
  ...), 25 places in all; use Cast<T>, which other V8 code uses for the
  same objects. Both only check the type in a DCHECK.

Status: verified at V8 f7be3c62 (Chromium 4558926c) in the iOS 27
simulator, with v8_drumbrake_bounds_checks = true and --wasm-jitless: a
test page covering arithmetic, memory, tables, tail calls, exceptions
(including WebAssembly.JSTag), GC structs and arrays, SIMD and two C
programs compiled with clang passes, and a try_table module fails with
a CompileError instead of crashing.

